# Lesson 6, batch 2 (2026-11-28): top of a long sequence against 3NT.
title: Queen from a long sequence
id: L06-17
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.3, 6.5
student: S
board: 17

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  K3     JT643  432     KJT
  E  A852   A97    AQJ5    84
  S  QJT94  852    K6      973
  W  76     KQ     T987    AQ652

auction:
  N Pass   = Not enough to open
  E 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
  S Pass   = Nothing to say
  W 3NT    = 10-14 points, no 4-card major: let's play game
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E Pass   = Happy with game
  S Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1NT (15-17) and West raised straight to 3NT. East is declarer,
  and so you, South, will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 15-17 = East's 1NT showed 15 to 17.
  dummy: 10-14 = West went straight to game opposite 1NT: 10 to 14.
  partner: 3-9 = 40, minus your 6, East's 15 to 17 and West's 10 to 14, leaves 3 to 9.
  remind: At no-trump the play is a race to set up long suits, and the first move is yours.

play:
  1   S SQ*  W S6  N SK  E SA
      SQ key: Your longest suit, with a sequence on top. The ♠Q gives nothing away and pushes out declarer's high spades, so the rest of your spades become winners.
      SQ not S: Right suit! With the queen, jack and ten on top, lead the queen.
      SQ not H: A heart is a guess. Which of your suits is longest, with a sequence on top?
      SQ not D: Your ♦K is your way back in later, once your spades are set up. Which suit could give you extra tricks?
      SQ not C: A club is a guess. Which of your suits is longest, with a sequence on top?
      note: Partner plays his king, so as not to block your suit, and declarer wins the ace. Your spades are now set up.
  2   E H7  S H2  W HK  N H3
      note: Declarer crosses to dummy's ♥K...
  3   W DT  N D2  E D5  S DK
      note: ...and tries the diamond finesse. Your king wins, and you have spades to cash.
  4   S SJ  W S7  N S3  E S2
      note: Run your spades.
  5   S ST  W C2  N H4  E S5
  6   S S9  W C5  N H6  E S8
  7   S S4  W C6  N D3  E C4
      note: Five tricks: 3NT is down one.
  8   S H8  W HQ  N HT  E H9
      note: You get out with a heart, and declarer takes the rest.
  9   W D9  N D4  E DJ  S D6
  10  E DA  S H5  W D8  N HJ
  11  E DQ  S C3  W D7  N CT
  12  E HA  S C7  W CQ  N CJ
  13  E C8  S C9  W CA  N CK

play recap:
  Your ♠Q knocked out declarer's ace at trick one, and partner helped by
  throwing his king under it, so the suit wasn't blocked. When the diamond
  finesse lost to your king, your spades were ready: four spade tricks and
  the ♦K, down one. Lead anything else, and declarer has time to set up the
  diamonds and clubs while your spades sit idle. A long suit with a sequence
  on top is the ideal lead against no-trump.
